[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its reports show two problems, and
the queued fix reaches only one of them. Each problem now has its own ticket.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} {filed} />

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser does not close the hint ticket. Keep the hint ticket open after the parser fix ships.
</Callout>

## The two problems

**[Parser](https://github.com/codeSanja/s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1).** The shared parser reads
every date month first, and these tenants write the day first. A date comes out with day and month
swapped. The fix is to parse by the tenant's locale.

**[Hint](https://github.com/codeSanja/s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2).** The `booking-v3` form tells
people to type the month first, then reads the date day first. Only reports through that form belong
here. The same tenants' app and email reports go through the parser. The fix is to make the hint
match how the form reads the date.

## How to tell them apart

Read the tenant and the channel, not the subject or the intake tag. Both problems produce the same
subjects, because the subject describes what the reporter saw. The intake tag is a guess from the
browser, as the "tagged as" column shows. `causes.ts` holds the rule, and
[the triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) lets you try it. Its subject-matching rule is how these two
problems ended up in one ticket.

## Still open

The desk notes list reports that read as a one-day move, or as two clients in different places
getting different days. The table's footnote counts them. They are filed under the parser by tenant,
but a day-month swap cannot move a date by one day, and this export does not show their cause. Check
them after the parser fix: if they still happen, they need their own ticket.

